\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Astarte\'92s myth is not preserved in a single, continuous story like the Greek myths of Aphrodite or Athena; instead, her \'93myth\'94 must be reconstructed from many scattered texts in which she appears as a goddess of love, war, and royal power.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jessicacaldas.squarespace.com/s/Transformation-of-a-goddess-astarte.pdf"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 jessicacaldas.squarespace+2}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Core identity and roles\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Astarte is a West Semitic goddess, closely related to (and sometimes identified with) Ishtar/Inanna, associated with sexuality, fertility, and 
\f1\b warfare
\f0\b0 .{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.encyclopedia.com/environment/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/eblaite-religion"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 encyclopedia+1}}\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In Phoenician and Punic religion she is a major goddess, often called \'93Lady\'94 or \'93Holy Queen,\'94 and linked with kingship, oaths, and protection in battle.[{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.encyclopedia.com/philosophy-and-religion/ancient-religions/ancient-religion/astarte"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 encyclopedia}}] \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Over time she becomes part of a broader \'93Great Goddess\'94 type in the eastern Mediterranean, overlapping with deities like Anat, Isis, and Aphrodite.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sacred-texts.com/cla/luc/tsg/tsg04.htm"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 sacred-texts+1}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 In Syrian and Ugaritic texts\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In Late Bronze Age Syria (Ugarit, Emar), Astarte (\uc0\u8219 Athtart) appears as a huntress and warrior goddess, present in ritual and administrative texts and participating in royal and cultic life rather than starring in long heroic narratives.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.academia.edu/12709064/_Athtart_in_Late_Bronze_Age_Syrian_Texts"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 academia+1}}\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ugaritic mythology hints that she belongs to the same circle as Baal and Anat and may support the storm\uc0\u8209 god in his struggles, emphasizing her martial and protective side.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.academia.edu/12709064/_Athtart_in_Late_Bronze_Age_Syrian_Texts"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 academia+1}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Love, sexuality, and fertility\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Like Ishtar, Astarte embodies powerful, often dangerous sexuality and human and animal fertility, and her cult centers on offerings meant to secure prosperity, offspring, and the renewal of life.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.encyclopedia.com/environment/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/eblaite-religion"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 encyclopedia+1}}\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In Phoenician cities she is the patroness of life\uc0\u8209 giving forces of nature\'97growth of crops, success in breeding, and the erotic dimension of human relationships\'97while still retaining her warlike aspects.[{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.encyclopedia.com/philosophy-and-religion/ancient-religions/ancient-religion/astarte"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 encyclopedia}}] \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 War, kingship, and the state\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Astarte is invoked in treaties, royal inscriptions, and temple texts as a divine guarantor of oaths and protector of the king and city in war.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sacred-texts.com/cla/luc/tsg/tsg04.htm"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 sacred-texts+1}}\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Her role as a royal patroness is visible where kings and queens bear names derived from hers or hold priestly titles in her service, indicating that she stands behind dynastic legitimacy and military success.[{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.encyclopedia.com/philosophy-and-religion/ancient-religions/ancient-religion/astarte"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 encyclopedia}}] \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Later reinterpretations\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In the Hebrew Bible, Astarte (Ashtoreth) appears as a foreign goddess whose cult some Israelites adopt, reflecting real Phoenician influence but presenting her negatively as a rival to Yahweh.[{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.encyclopedia.com/philosophy-and-religion/ancient-religions/ancient-religion/astarte"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 encyclopedia}}] \
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 In the Greco\uc0\u8209 Roman world, Astarte becomes identified with Aphrodite and other great goddesses, and her myths are absorbed into broader traditions about a powerful heavenly Queen who governs love, war, and fate.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sacred-texts.com/cla/luc/tsg/tsg04.htm"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 sacred-texts+1}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 In sum, the \'93myth behind Astarte\'94 is the story of a powerful Semitic goddess who unites sexuality, fertility, and violent strength, stands beside kings as their patron, and gradually merges with other great goddesses of the ancient Mediterranean rather than being known from a single, fixed legend.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.academia.edu/12709064/_Athtart_in_Late_Bronze_Age_Syrian_Texts"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 academia+2}}\
}